WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:05.180
Automatic patterning on our knitting
machines is almost magical.

2
00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:08.860
Do you remember the first time that you
played with stitch patterning?

3
00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:14.980
I remember crawling under my machine
and looking back to see the results.

4
00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:18.820
But often we make things harder
than they really need to be.

5
00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:23.820
Many a knitter has gotten discouraged
because of all those buttons and levers,

6
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:27.620
and it just seemed too complex,
complicated and overwhelming.

7
00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:33.100
We're presented with diagrams that look
like this or a bunch of translated

8
00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:37.020
Japanese in a manual
that really isn't clear.

9
00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:41.740
No matter what machine you're using,
there are really only three things

10
00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:46.820
to learn and understand when it
comes to automatic patterning.

11
00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:51.620
Let's consider a manual knitting
machine for just a minute.

12
00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:53.380
In order to create, say,

13
00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:57.820
tuck on a manual machine,
you'll manually pull out some

14
00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:01.900
of the needles all the way and then
set your carriage to hold.

15
00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:03.940
As you push your carriage across,

16
00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:08.850
some needles will form stitches
and other needles won't knit at all.

17
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.700
You'll do this three times and then cancel

18
00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:17.490
hold, knit across and you've
created a series of tuck stitches.

19
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.100
Are you with me?

20
00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:21.340
So two things happened.

21
00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:27.020
Needles were positioned by you and the
carriage was set in a certain way.

22
00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.700
Now, let's take this a step further.

23
00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:35.210
The first puzzle piece is the automatic
needle selection mechanism.

24
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:36.850
Oh, that's a mouthful.

25
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.140
Let's call him OTTO.

26
00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.620
We're going to tell him what to do.

27
00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:43.540
Automatic patterning machines have

28
00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:49.420
a mechanism that cuts you out of the loop.
To form stitch patterns.

29
00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:53.180
needles are selected automatically.

30
00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:56.380
How does OTTO know what needles to select?

31
00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:59.020
Well, he isn't really very smart.

32
00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:03.820
He relies on instructions
from punch card or electronics.

33
00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:10.170
Hole / no hole, on/off, black
square/ whitesquare, ones/zeros.

34
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.610
Auto only knows on and off.

35
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:17.940
Now, once upon a time,
machines had actual mechanical switches

36
00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:22.140
that you could turn on and off
to create stitch patterns.

37
00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:23.660
No matter how you do it,

38
00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:29.220
flipping a switch, reading a punch card or
electronics translates to positioning

39
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:35.170
the needles as you knit
to the correct position for patterning.

40
00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.780
So needle position is
puzzle piece number two.

41
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.780
Now, we don't have to worry too much about

42
00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:45.740
this because OTTO takes care
of the positioning of needles for us.

43
00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:50.020
Moving on the automatic
needle selection mechanism.

44
00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:58.300
OTTO has read the ons and offs and he's
selecting positioning needles for us.

45
00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:02.610
But how does he know what
stitch pattern we want to knit?

46
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:08.740
As I said, the device isn't really very
smart and it certainly doesn't read minds.

47
00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:14.340
The third piece of the puzzle is
the carriage or cam settings.

48
00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.980
It's the carriage that pushes those

49
00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:21.940
needles back and forth,
forming stitches as we knit.

50
00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:27.300
All of the different brands of machines
call these settings something different.

51
00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:32.300
And the buttons and levers are all located
in different places on the carriage.

52
00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:37.820
This gets especially confusing when you
work with different knitting machines.

53
00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:41.420
Now, if you have one machine or multiple
machines,

54
00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:47.660
the secret is to understand the function
of the cam levers or buttons

55
00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:56.300
and their relationship to the other two
puzzle pieces OTTO and needle positions.

56
00:03:56.330 --> 00:04:02.300
Your challenge is to locate
and identify the OTTO on your machine.

57
00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:07.300
Introduce yourself to OTTO and appreciate
the fact that he's keeping track

58
00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:12.740
of the stitch patterns
and pulling needles for you.

59
00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:17.060
Be aware of the different needle
positions and their function.

60
00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:20.660
A-B-C-D-E-F OTTO is going to select

61
00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:26.460
the needles for you and the cam levers
and buttons will control those needles.

62
00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.220
But if you have to rip or bind off or

63
00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:36.400
troubleshoot in some way, it's critical
that you understand the needle positions.

64
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:41.300
Lastly, your challenge is to identify
the specific cam buttons or levers

65
00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:47.100
that control the needle movement
to create specific stitch patterns.

66
00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.380
For example, once you have the first two

67
00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:54.180
puzzle pieces in place,
which button or lever do you set to knit

68
00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:55.420
fairisle?

69
00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:59.580
What about tuck? On some of the machines

70
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.380
this is pretty clear.

71
00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:04.580
This clearly tells me
this is my tuck setting.

72
00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.460
But part what does that mean?

73
00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:10.820
Maybe there's just a symbol.

74
00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.660
You need to translate for yourself

75
00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:20.040
the settings for the different
stitch techniques on your machine.

76
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:28.860
The very best way to cement these concepts
for yourself is practice and repetition.

77
00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:35.100
Step one meet OTTO. What's
his name on your machine?

78
00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:38.580
Step two understand the needle positions.

79
00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:42.900
ABCDEF, the letter really doesn't matter.

80
00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:49.860
You need to understand what happens
in each of the positions as you knit.

81
00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:53.060
Step three identify and learn each

82
00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:56.660
of the settings for each
of the available stitch techniques.

83
00:05:56.690 --> 00:06:00.900
Tuck, slip, fairisle weaving,
lace, thread lace.

84
00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:07.220
All of the beautiful stitch patterns
we can create automatically.

85
00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:12.580
Understanding the three steps will put you
in the right track for success

86
00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:17.820
with patterning on your
machine with less gray hair.

87
00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:18.600
Happy knitting.

